# One line ending, on every machine.
#
# Git on Windows checks text files out with CRLF unless it is told otherwise,
# and this project is written on Windows as much as anywhere. A program that
# reads a committed file and searches it for a newline then reads bytes the
# writer never wrote: the lesson stories are found in AudioNarrator.jsx by
# searching for "\n  <number>: {" and the same search finds "\r\n" instead, so
# the build fails on a machine that checked the repository out the ordinary
# Windows way while the same commit builds on Linux. This file removes the
# difference between the two machines rather than teaching every reader about
# it, which is one rule here against one `\r` to remember in each of them.
#
# The batch file is the one text file that keeps its CRLF: `cmd.exe` reads it,
# and the format it expects is the one Windows writes.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.bat text eol=crlf
